ttps://www.goodreads.com/review/show/1999414287">May 14, 2017</a></span></section><section class="ReviewText"><section class="ReviewText__content" dir="auto"><div class="TruncatedContent" tabindex="-1"><div class="TruncatedContent__text TruncatedContent__text--large" tabindex="-1" data-testid="contentContainer"><span class="Formatted">I voluntarily reviewed an Advance Reader Copy of this book. This story started out dark—kind of like her Blue Moon Saloon spinoff—but like that spinoff of the Twin Moon Ranch stories, it is just the build-up for much better things to come! In this story, we meet lonely and somewhat doubtful Boone, cousin to Ty and Cody who encounters his fated mate washed up on the beach near his bungalow with a head injury and no real memory of who she is or why she ended up in the water. Nina is sweet, strong, resilient, and intuitive. She’s very likeable. It’s obvious how likeable she is when she is bestowed with a gift of enormous value. She even manages to take the edge off of Cruz, the resident tiger shifter. We meet villains Kramer, a mercenary wolf shifter and Tamara, a witch that Boone once thought was his fated mate. They are thoroughly despicable and from the moment you meet them, you almost hope that something truly bad happens to them. This story has everything—lust, love, hope, redemption, suspense, a sense of self, and even a part that will make you sad. Hunter, the grizzly shifter has so much on his soul. I really can’t wait for his story and Officer Meli. She strikes me as a very interesting character, who aside from being model beautiful, seems to have her own reasons for becoming a police officer and doing what others least expect her to do—and I can’t wait!</span></div><div class=""></div></div></section></section><section class="ReviewCard__tags"><ul class="CollapsableList"><span tabindex="-1"></span><span tabindex="-1"></span></ul></section><footer class="SocialFooter"><div data-testid="actions"><div class="SocialFooter__action"><